    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Done. All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test file, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required de-duplicating an owner's computed `customerCode` against existing owners: on collision, append `-<n>` with the smallest `n \u2265 2` that makes it unique.\n\nTo keep structural impact low I avoided growing the large `OwnerRestControllerV1` class or the `CustomerCode` class. Instead:\n\n1. **New `UniqueCustomerCode` util class** (`src/main/java/.../util/UniqueCustomerCode.java`) \u2014 a small cohesive unit with near-zero surrounding complexity (`WMC_other \u2248 0`). It calls the existing `CustomerCode.assign` for the base code, then loops appending `-<n>` (n starting at 2) until unique.\n\n2. **One-line change** in the controller's `addOwner` \u2014 swapped `CustomerCode.assign(owner)` for `UniqueCustomerCode.assign(owner, findAllOwners())`, plus the corresponding import. Only a single line of that big method changed (`changed_lines = 1`), keeping its costly `WMC_other` contribution minimal.\n\nAll the collision logic lives in the new class where `WMC_other` is 1, so the dominant cost term is avoided entirely.",
